Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
uh very pleased to say well once again  joined in the studio by the foreign  secretary dominic wrap mr rabb good to  see you this morning  um clearly there is an awful lot to  discuss in terms of afghanistan moving  forward but but we do need to to go back  to to last week to the tail end of last  week on friday kabul was was we now know  48 hours from being taken over by the  taliban you spoke to the prime minister  from your holiday on friday what was the  content of that conversation well look  i'm not going to go into all of the  details those are private conversations  but what we were overwhelmingly focused  on with the cobra meetings the meetings  i had with the director running the  emergency response and indeed the  international engagement that i was  focused on is two things securing the  airport so that flights evacuation  flights could come in and out secondly  boosting our teams on the ground we now  have 19 foreign office officials many  home office officials so we can process  not just the uk nationals but the  afghans who worked for us and i would  just say this if you look from that date  forward over 9 000 people have been uh  given safe passage to the uk i don't  think there's been an air lift on that  scale that level of complexity and  living memory ended and we will we will  dwell on that as well during this  interview but you were three days away  on friday from from making your way back  to the united kingdom  on sunday evening  indeed sorry apologies to  what was the intelligence assessment on  friday of how long it would take the  taliban to take over the capital of  afghanistan well i don't go into the  specific intelligence but but we not we  don't discuss intelligence matters and  operational assessments but i can tell  you this uh and it's been clear all  along clearly the scale and pace  of the fall of kabul came as a surprise  to everyone around the world not just  western powers like the uk and the us  but when i was speaking to the qataris  the pakistanis i think even the taliban  were surprised but of course we can  learn those lessons with hindsight we  dealt with the reality that we saw it  but in terms of the the conversation  that you had with the prime minister had  you been ordered to come back to the  united kingdom to be at your desk for  the fall of of kabul did you lobby the  prime minister to be to work remotely  all of those claims and suggestions  we discussed the matter i agreed to come  back on sunday i'm not going to add  further uh to the speculation around  that but the idea uh some of the  reporting some of the assertions you put  to me just not true but we were focused  overwhelmingly on securing the airport  making sure and i was engaged in  meetings that the the stuff about me  being lounging around the beach all day  just nonsense  the stuff about me paddleboarding  nonsense the sea was actually closed it  was a red notice i was focused on the  cobra meetings the foreign office team  the director and the director general  and the international engagement and as  a result of the actions of the  government 9 000 people  british nationals afghan workers were  secured back to the uk but the reason  this is significant is demonstrated by a  poll in the mail this morning which  shows a majority of people want you to  lose your job for not coming back from  your holiday the fact that as the  commons debate demonstrated there are  people within your own party who feel  the government was disinterested in what  was going on in the ground you were on  holiday the prime minister was on  holiday the permanent secretary of the  foreign office was on holiday the  minister responsible for afghanistan was  on holiday  self-selecting reader polls in uh  newspapers i'm afraid uh are not a  particularly accurate barometer um but  in terms of what we're doing at all  material times because foreign secretary  of course with the benefit of hindsight  i wouldn't have gone away but that's a  luxury of commentators not politicians  and if you're the foreign secretary i'm  traveling all the time  for work purposes i always need to be  able to grip  crises that arise and that's what we  were doing do you regret not coming back  earlier look of course i said with the  benefit of hindsight i'd have been back  earlier but we were all surprised by the  scale and the pace of uh the collapse of  the situation in kabul and as i said  i was still doing all the things you  would need me to do to focus on what  really mattered engaging in the cobra  meetings but critically securing ramping  up the foreign office presence in kabul  and working with the mod ben wallis the  home secretary to make sure we could not  just secure the airport but get the  people home we got over 9 000 people  home since that point in time and i do  think that demonstrates that we were on  the job just in terms of of where we are  with the repatriation course it's  reported this morning that you've  briefed mps that that in essence all  british nationals all mono nationals  people with just single british  citizenship are basically out of the  country is that accurate so there's a  whole range of cases but basically uh  mono nationals so single nationality uk  who have got documentation the line  share almost all of them that want to  come out have been  brought home the ones that are remaining  and we're we've done an amazing job two  and a half thousand uk nationals if you  go back to april and remember it was in  april it's not just all starting in  august where we said you must leave the  country you should leave the country  will support you to do so so we've seen  two and a half thousand british  nationals come back what remains are  rather complex cases large family units  where one or other may be documented or  maybe a clearly national but it's not  clear whether the rest of them are so  we've got a great team on the ground in  kabul and in king charles street in the  fcdo checking and getting all of those  residual cases on where they're eligible  obviously again for intelligence reasons  you will not be able to put a specific  time on when we will be out of  afghanistan i understand that there are  however suggestions  that the withdrawal could begin in as  little as 24 to 48 hours can you comment  on that well look for precisely the  reason you gave i'm not going to give  the precise time and what we do know is  that we're working towards the end of  the month the military planners will  work out how much time they need to  withdraw their equipment their staff and  what what's really important is we'll  make the maximum use of all the time we  have left just to give you a sense now  in the last 24 hours i checked the  numbers coming in we've secured  2 000 back to the uk so the system is  operating at full speed  at full capacity and we'll use every  last remaining hour and day to get  everyone we can back the british  nationals the afghans who work so  loyally for us we're getting the  achieving scholars back uh also women's  rights defenders and journalists indeed  um but but just in terms of the timeline  given that everyone from the prime  minister down i think you've said this  as well has has basically made it clear  that our our military presence on the  ground in kabul is predicated on the  americans being there as well we will  have to come out before they do  it is fair to say isn't it then on the  basis of that timeline that we may well  be out by the end of the weekend well we  will work to the end of august but we'll  take back from that you step back from  that the time that we need to withdraw  our military operation uh the military  plans are firming that up i can't give  you the precise details um because we  want to we want to make sure we use  every last hour and day to keep this  rate up and we've done an and again i  just stressed the teams on the ground  and in king charles street at the  emergency response center did an amazing  job since april since april over 11 000  people given safe passage back to the uk  it's never been done before and no  country has done better we know that  there will be people who will not be  able to make it back before the august  31st deadline again suggestions this  morning that in some fashion kabul  international airport could continue to  see people leaving on civilian flights  so we had conversations with the taliban  about the possibility of this we do  engage with the taliban militarily uh on  the ground and in doha with the  political representation i think the uh  we would we would like to see a kabul  airport go back to being functional that  will require on the security the the  security on the ground it'll require it  to be done safely and of course it'll  require the taliban to live up to their  assurances about allowing safe passage  out they've actually so far  tried to be constructive as we've seen  with the numbers we've got and tried to  be constructed in their own way  and what we've then got to do is test  them  beyond the withdrawal date will they  still allow safe passage as they've  undertaken will they allow humanitarian  groups uh the permissive environment to  be able to operate  so there is a next stage of engagement  not recognition engagement with the  taliban we will hold them very clearly  to the assurances that they're already  stating but we heard it yesterday though  the the head of the cia traveled to  kabul to meet with the taliban directly  has something similar happened with the  head of mi6 as richard moore held any  meetings with the taliban richard  moore's the head of mi6 not mi5  but we will engage diplomatically as  well as through the military presence on  the ground i won't say too much more but  i do think what i would say now is it's  important that we can convey our  messages directly and one of the ways  that we've been able to handle this  pretty uh epic  evacuation is by being able to engage  and convey clear messages i do think  that's important and we want to make  sure there's some continuity to that but  just can you at least give us a sense  that the uk spy chief is engaging  directly with the taliban trying to help  secure the mission i'm not going to  comment on either c or the natural depth  of our engagement but as i mentioned of  course on the ground in relation to the  airport we engage we with their their  british troops uh the taliban cheek and  gel and also via the political  commission which is based in doa we make  sure that we can engage speaking to your  colleague again ben wallace yesterday i  mean he made it pretty clear obviously  the security situation around the  airport is is pretty severe  and the closer we get to august the 31st  the greater the risk of of some form of  attack on or on the allied forces there  is it your understanding that islamic  states al-qaeda any of those other  groups are are extant in afghanistan at  the moment are operational in  afghanistan i think we're also concerned  about the way the prisons were emptied  by the taliban so of course that means  that we're  ever vigilant about that potential risk  clearly one of the key things that we're  saying to the taliban is first of all to  continue the safe passage of people out  british nationals and others  we're they're saying the taliban  spokesman said yesterday he wanted to  avoid the brain drain well actually in  that case they're going to need to be a  lot more tolerant and inclusive than  they've been before um  and of course the number one thing for  us beyond safe passage we'll be making  sure afghanistan is never used as a base  for terrorism again and the taliban has  said that they're committed to that  what are the levers we can use to exert  some form of influence over the taliban  you yourself have written about the  potential  for sanctions we know however that  perhaps as much as 60 percent of the  afghan state budget comes from money  from from overseas if we are to turn off  the taps in terms of the money we will  see an even greater humanitarian  catastrophe won't we there is look  there's always the risk with sanctions  that you need to target them in a way  that protects the civilians and and  deals with the individuals that you want  to hold to account there's a whole range  of things uh the amount of aid that  they'll need  the access to the international  financial system billions of pounds the  taliban are saying they do not want  whole economy and social uh fabric such  as it is in afghanistan to collapse that  gives us leverage on them in relation to  those kiosks safe passage out  never to allow afghanistan to be used  for terrorism and indeed the permissive  environment that they will need to  provide if they want the un ngos to  operate in afghanistan if they don't  we're not going to give aid the taliban  we would only do it via those third  parties  of course the g7 yesterday made it very  clear look taken together we have an  awful lot of economic and diplomatic  clout however look at those countries  which still have an embassy open on the  ground there russia china if afghanistan  strikes bilateral deals with them  that does make it rather more difficult  for the g7 to exact settlements doesn't  it well first of all i think that pm was  absolutely right take the lead use the  g7 presidency get the g7 together and  the statement was uh pretty thorough a  starting point for the way forward but  you're right we need a broader group of  countries i've been speaking to my  chinese opposite number  we are pressing the russians as well and  indeed the countries in the region i've  spoken to the indian the pakistani  foreign ministers if we want to exert  maximum moderating influence on the  taliban such as it is with all due  skepticism we're going to need to  operate in a greater a broader corpus  that's one of the reasons why we're  pressing for a meeting of the permanent  five  members of the security council which  includes china russia as well as france  the uk and the u.s in the coming days to  provide at least the contours of a way  forward that we can all agree on uh a  lot for you to be getting on with so  we'll let you go on with the domino  graph good to talk to you

